    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Choose a $(-1)$-truncated object $\widetilde{U} \in \calX $ representing $U$. Since $\pi ^{\ast }$ is left exact, an object $X$ of $\calX /U$ is $(-1)$-truncated as an object of $\calX /U$ if and only if it is $(-1)$-truncated as an object of $\calX $. It therefore suffices to prove that if $\widetilde{V}$ is a $(-1)$-truncated object of $\calX $ representing an element $V \in \Sub (1_{\calX })$, then $\widetilde{V}$ is $S_{U}$-local if and only if $U \subseteq V$. One direction is clear: if $\widetilde{V}$ is $S_ U$-local, then we have an isomorphism 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{\calX }( \widetilde{U}, \widetilde{V} ) \rightarrow \bHom _{\calX }( \emptyset , \widetilde{V}) = \ast  \]
</div>
    <p>
       in the homotopy category $\calH $, so that $U \subseteq V$. The converse follows from characterization $(3)$ given in Lemma <a href="/tag/E3E303" data-tag="E3E303">7.3.2.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
